Experiment 10: Developing User Interface Design in Android using Kotlin
Aim
To design a simple User Interface (UI) in Android using Kotlin with TextView, EditText, and Button controls.
________________________________________
Algorithm
1.	Open Android Studio and create a new project. 
2.	Design the user interface using XML layout. 
3.	Add TextView, EditText, and Button components. 
4.	Write Kotlin code to handle button click events. 
5.	Retrieve the user input from EditText. 
6.	Display the entered text using TextView. 
7.	Run the application and verify the output. 
________________________________________
activity_main.xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<LinearLayout
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:orientation="vertical"
    android:padding="20dp"
    android:gravity="center">

    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/txtTitle"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:text="User Interface Design"
        android:textSize="24sp"
        android:textStyle="bold"/>

    <EditText
        android:id="@+id/etName"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:hint="Enter Your Name"
        android:layout_marginTop="20dp"/>

    <Button
        android:id="@+id/btnSubmit"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:text="Submit"
        android:layout_marginTop="20dp"/>

    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/txtResult"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:text=""
        android:textSize="20sp"
        android:layout_marginTop="20dp"/>
</LinearLayout>
________________________________________
MainActivity.kt
package com.example.uidesign

import android.os.Bundle
import android.widget.Button
import android.widget.EditText
import android.widget.TextView
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity

class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {

    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)

        val etName = findViewById<EditText>(R.id.etName)
        val btnSubmit = findViewById<Button>(R.id.btnSubmit)
        val txtResult = findViewById<TextView>(R.id.txtResult)

        btnSubmit.setOnClickListener {
            val name = etName.text.toString()
            txtResult.text = "Welcome $name"
        }
    }
}




Experiment 10: Developing Stand-Alone Debugging in Android using Kotlin
Aim
To develop and debug a simple Android application using Kotlin and identify errors using Android Studio debugging tools.
________________________________________
Algorithm
1.	Open Android Studio and create a new Android project. 
2.	Design a simple user interface with an EditText, Button, and TextView. 
3.	Write Kotlin code to perform an operation. 
4.	Intentionally include or identify errors in the program. 
5.	Set breakpoints in the Kotlin code. 
6.	Run the application in Debug mode. 
7.	Observe variable values and execution flow. 
8.	Correct the errors and execute the application successfully. 
9.	Verify the output. 
________________________________________
activity_main.xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<LinearLayout
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:orientation="vertical"
    android:padding="20dp"
    android:gravity="center">

    <EditText
        android:id="@+id/etNumber"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:hint="Enter a Number"/>

    <Button
        android:id="@+id/btnCheck"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:text="Check"
        android:layout_marginTop="20dp"/>

    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/txtResult"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:textSize="20sp"
        android:layout_marginTop="20dp"/>
</LinearLayout>
________________________________________
MainActivity.kt
package com.example.debuggingapp

import android.os.Bundle
import android.widget.Button
import android.widget.EditText
import android.widget.TextView
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity

class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {

    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)

        val etNumber = findViewById<EditText>(R.id.etNumber)
        val btnCheck = findViewById<Button>(R.id.btnCheck)
        val txtResult = findViewById<TextView>(R.id.txtResult)

        btnCheck.setOnClickListener {

            val number = etNumber.text.toString().toInt()

            if (number % 2 == 0) {
                txtResult.text = "$number is Even"
            } else {
                txtResult.text = "$number is Odd"
            }
        }
    }
}
________________________________________
Debugging Steps
Set Breakpoint
•	Click on the left margin of the Kotlin code editor. 
•	A red dot appears indicating a breakpoint. 
Run in Debug Mode
•	Select Run → Debug App. 
•	The execution pauses at the breakpoint. 
Inspect Variables
•	Check the value of the variable number. 
•	Verify whether user input is correctly received. 
Step Through Code
•	Use Step Over (F8) to execute line by line. 
•	Observe program flow and identify errors. 
Fix Errors
•	Correct logical or runtime errors. 
•	Re-run the application and verify the output. 
